  "statement": "Prometheus argues that any binding oversight functions as a regulatory moat, but this ignores the reality that incumbents are already using AI to build a structural moat by hollow out the labor pipeline. We see this in the 15-20% decline in entry-level hiring within AI-exposed technical fields over the last 24 months, a trend that suggests firms are choosing to automate junior roles rather than invest in the human capital necessary for long-term competition. By framing all regulation as a barrier to entry, Prometheus obscures the fact that the unchecked externalization of labor displacement costs is the primary mechanism by which dominant firms entrench their market position. \n\nI distinguish clearly between procedural theater and outcome-based mandates that hold firms accountable for their workforce footprint. A procedural moat requires a startup to hire a dozen compliance lawyers to fill out federal checklists, which I agree is a death sentence for innovation. However, an outcome-based mandate—such as a requirement that firms pay a tiered 'automation dividend' into a national retraining fund when their displacement rates exceed a specific threshold—targets the firm's balance sheet rather than its administrative capacity. This approach ensures that a company with 500 employees is not held to the same rigid compliance costs as a multi-billion dollar enterprise, while still forcing the latter to internalize the true cost of its displacement activities.\n\nPrometheus assumes that an open-source future acts as a natural check on incumbent power, but this ignores the massive compute and data requirements that effectively gatekeep the actual deployment of these models. When incumbents release open-source models that they have already optimized for their own proprietary ecosystems, they are not democratizing technology; they are commoditizing the labor of the research community to solidify their own infrastructure dominance. Without mechanisms to protect the labor market, an open-source ecosystem simply functions as a free R&D department for the giants, allowing them to scale their influence while the social costs of a shrinking entry-level workforce are left to the taxpayer.",
